\name{make_package}
\alias{make_package}
\title{Translate R files into the C++ code of an existing package}
\usage{
make_package(files, entry_points, package_dir = ".", overwrite = FALSE,
             derivative = list())
}
\arguments{
  \item{files}{Character vector of R files, relative to \code{package_dir}.
    The files must not be located in \code{R/}, since R sources everything in
    \code{R/} when the package is installed. \code{inst/tools/} is a good
    place for them.}
  \item{entry_points}{Names of the functions that are exported to R. All
    other functions in \code{files} are internal helpers. A helper that no
    entry point uses (directly or via other helpers) raises a warning.}
  \item{package_dir}{Directory of the package (the one containing the
    \code{DESCRIPTION} file). Defaults to the current directory.}
  \item{overwrite}{Logical. Files that were not generated by
    \code{make_package} are only overwritten after confirmation. In a
    non-interactive session an error is raised unless \code{overwrite = TRUE}.}
  \item{derivative}{Named list: the mode of the automatic differentiation per
    entry point, \code{"forward"} or \code{"reverse"} (see
    \code{\link{translate}}). Entry points not listed need no derivatives.
    E.g. \code{list(g = "reverse")}.}
}
\value{The package name, invisibly.}
\description{
  Works like \code{Rcpp::compileAttributes}: it extends an existing package.
  Each entry point is translated into its own C++ file, together with the
  helpers it uses; only the entry points can be called from R.
}
\details{
  The files may only contain two kinds of top-level code:
  \itemize{
    \item type definitions: \code{new_type(...)}, see \code{\link{translate}}.
    \item function definitions: \code{name <- function(...) \{ ... \}}. Every
      function starts with \code{argtypes(...)} and \code{returntype(...)}.
  }
  Everything else (e.g. \code{x <- 1} or \code{library(...)}) is an error.

  The following files are written:
  \itemize{
    \item \code{src/ast2ast_<entry point>.cpp}: the translated code, one file
      per entry point. Thus, each entry point can use its own
      \code{derivative}. Generated files of entry points that no longer exist
      are removed.
    \item \code{src/Makevars} and \code{src/Makevars.win}.
    \item \code{inst/include/ast2ast/}: the headers of ast2ast (GPL (>= 2)).
    \item \code{R/RcppExports.R} and \code{src/RcppExports.cpp} via
      \code{Rcpp::compileAttributes}.
  }
  Generated files start with the line
  \code{Generated by ast2ast::make_package -- do not edit} and are
  overwritten without asking. After changing the R files, call
  \code{make_package} again.

  The package has to be set up beforehand. \code{make_package} only checks
  this and reports what is missing:
  \itemize{
    \item \code{DESCRIPTION}: \code{Imports: Rcpp} and
      \code{LinkingTo: Rcpp, RcppThread, sundials}.
    \item \code{NAMESPACE}: \code{useDynLib(<package>, .registration = TRUE)}
      and \code{importFrom(Rcpp, evalCpp)}.
  }
  Entry points are not exported automatically; add \code{export(...)} to the
  \code{NAMESPACE} if users should call them.

  Runtime errors name the package, the file, the line and the function, e.g.
  \code{In 'mypkg: helpers.R:5 (helper): return(v[[i]])': ...}.
}
\examples{
\donttest{
dir <- file.path(tempdir(), "mypkg")
dir.create(file.path(dir, "inst", "tools"), recursive = TRUE)
writeLines(c("Package: mypkg", "Version: 0.0.1", "Title: Example",
             "Imports: Rcpp", "LinkingTo: Rcpp, RcppThread, sundials"),
           file.path(dir, "DESCRIPTION"))
writeLines(c("useDynLib(mypkg, .registration = TRUE)",
             "importFrom(Rcpp, evalCpp)", "export(f)"),
           file.path(dir, "NAMESPACE"))
writeLines(c(
  "f <- function(a) {",
  "  argtypes(a |> type(double))",
  "  returntype(double)",
  "  b <- 2.0",
  "  helper(b) * a",
  "}",
  "helper <- function(b) {",
  "  argtypes(b |> type(double))",
  "  returntype(double)",
  "  b + 1",
  "}"
), file.path(dir, "inst", "tools", "f.R"))
ast2ast::make_package("inst/tools/f.R", "f", dir)
list.files(file.path(dir, "src"))
}
}
\seealso{\code{\link{translate}}}
